          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Ryobi Snow Blower",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/ryobi-snow-blower/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Ryobi make an 18V snow blower?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Ryobi's 18V ONE+ snow tool is a 10-inch snow shovel, listed with a 10-inch path, 6-inch depth and a 20- or 25-foot throw depending on the version. Its full-size snow blowers run on the 40V platform, because a driveway of snow needs more stored energy than a single 18V pack holds."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are Ryobi 18V and 40V batteries interchangeable?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. The 18V ONE+ and 40V systems use different batteries and chargers, so a ONE+ pack will not run a 40V snow blower. If you own only 18V tools, the snow shovel is the Ryobi snow tool that uses your batteries; a 40V blower means buying into the second platform."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How deep a snow can the Ryobi 40V single-stage handle?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Ryobi lists a 13-inch clearing depth, which is the height of the intake. In practice single-stage machines handle light and medium snow well and slow down in deep, wet snow and plow berms, where you take narrower passes or clear in stages during the storm."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is the Ryobi two-stage snow blower worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is worth it if you already own 40V batteries, have a long or gravel driveway or heavy snow, and want to stop maintaining a gas engine. Ryobi lists a 24-inch path, 21-inch depth, 55-foot throw and self-propelled drive. On price alone, a gas two-stage usually costs less up front."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a Ryobi snow blower run on a charge?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Ryobi quotes up to 45 minutes for the 24-inch two-stage on four 40V 6Ah batteries. Runtime drops in heavy, wet snow and in deep cold, so treat published figures as best cases and keep batteries warm indoors until you start."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a Ryobi snow blower be used on gravel?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not the single-stage. Its auger contacts the ground and will throw gravel. A two-stage machine keeps the auger above the surface and is the appropriate type for gravel driveways; check the manual for the correct skid shoe setting before using it on loose stone."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where are Ryobi snow blowers sold?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In the US, Ryobi's power tools are sold mainly through The Home Depot, and Ryobi's own site lists the models and kits. Configurations differ between retailers and seasons, so confirm the model number and the number and size of included batteries on the listing before you buy."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
